# graphify: commit graph.json + GRAPH_REPORT.md. .graphify_labels.json is
# committed only while it is already TRACKED here. Tracked-ness is the only
# durable signal this job has, and what it records is a deliberate decision to
# KEEP a labels file -- not that any name inside it was written by a human. A
# tracked file whose entries are all `Community N` placeholders, or all
# graphify auto-names, carries nothing to preserve, and the reindex job
# correctly lets the rebuild's machine names stand: carry-graphify-labels.py
# re-keys only an entry that is neither the placeholder nor one of that
# community's own member labels (BLO-40331, BLO-41817). An untracked copy is
# machine-generated and stays out of the index, so the first commit of this
# file is always a deliberate act.
# This file is the INPUT of record for community names; it is NOT the only
# committed copy of them. The same names are DERIVED into two other tracked
# places on every rebuild -- `community_name` on every graph.json node, and
# GRAPH_REPORT.md's `### Community N` headings -- and the reindex job rewrites
# all three together. So hand-edit this file and those two read stale until the
# next weekly rebuild re-keys them; hand-edit those two and the next rebuild
# overwrites them. Rename here, and only here.
# .graphify_labels.json.sig is graphify's membership-signature sidecar for that
# file (cli.py writes community_member_sigs() beside every labels persist). It
# is derived, rewritten on every rebuild, and its only consumer is graphify's
# own reuse path -- which DROPS a label whose community changed rather than
# re-keying it, so committing it would not preserve curated names. Ignore it.
# manifest.json is graphify's incremental-rebuild cache: this job rebuilds with
# --force from a fresh clone every run, so it is never read and a committed copy
# can only ever describe an older graph (NOP#1079).
cost.json
.graphify_python
.graphify_root
graph.html
.graphify_labels.json.sig
cache/
memory/
manifest.json
